          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Healthcare Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $53,305 median household income with a 45.5-year median age and 31% renter rate, ME-HD-7 is a community of older working adults whose healthcare costs and housing stability are primary financial concerns. The 21.1% college attainment rate signals a constituency without the credential-based income pathway that insulates higher-education communities from economic pressure. Rural Maine's healthcare access challenges — hospital viability, prescription drug costs, and Medicare sustainability — are the material governance issues that resonate with this demographic profile and are amenable to specific legislative remedies." },
          { name: "Rural Infrastructure & Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 45.5-year median age and 31% renter rate in a rural western Maine district indicate a mixed-tenure community where both homeowners and renters depend on reliable rural infrastructure: road maintenance, water and sewer systems, broadband access, and emergency services. The $53,305 income constrains household ability to absorb service gaps or cost increases. A representative who can translate specific infrastructure needs into state funding and regulatory clarity — and who understands the cost structure of rural service delivery — builds durable constituent relationships that transcend partisan margins." },
          { name: "Unopposed Status & Latent Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "Swallow ran unopposed in 2024 with a 100% Republican result — but this reflects absence of organized opposition, not depth of constituency engagement or consensus. In a district with a 45.5-year median age, working-class income profile, and mixed homeowner-renter composition, latent concerns about healthcare costs, housing affordability, and rural service cuts represent a coalition that could mobilize if a credible challenger emerges. An unopposed incumbent who governs as though the seat is safely held — rather than actively cultivating constituent relationships around the material concerns this demographic profile reflects — risks a competitive challenge in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Treating Rural Healthcare as a Secondary Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "For a 45.5-year-old constituency at $53,305 income in rural Maine, healthcare access and cost are not abstract policy debates — they are direct household budget constraints and genuine life-or-death governance issues. An incumbent who treats rural hospital viability, prescription drug affordability, or Medicare sustainability as peripheral to state governance rather than core constituent concerns signals misalignment with the district's actual priorities and creates an opening for an opponent who takes these issues seriously." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Positioning Without Constituent Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed 2024 result and absence of recent competitive pressure create a risk that the incumbent defaults to generic Republican messaging rather than grounding the legislative record in specific constituent benefits and problem-solving. In a district where 31% of households rent, healthcare costs are rising, and infrastructure reliability is uncertain, voters evaluate representatives on whether they have delivered concrete improvements to their material circumstances — not on fidelity to abstract partisan principles. A legislator who cannot articulate specific constituent problems solved and tangible improvements delivered is vulnerable to an opponent who can." }
