          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Institution Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 29% college attainment rate, while lower than many districts, represents Maine professionals whose economic interests depend on institutional funding — education systems, healthcare providers, research facilities, and professional employment. In a district where this 29% is currently consolidated with the homeowner majority, that consolidation is vulnerable if an incumbent's voting record is perceived as indifferent to education funding, healthcare system viability, or professional employment. College-educated voters in Maine have demonstrated capacity to swing when they perceive threats to the institutions their livelihoods depend on; active engagement on these specific issues strengthens the base rather than assuming their loyalty." },
          { name: "Healthcare System Viability for Aging Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "A 48.2 median age and 83% homeownership means a significant portion of HD-10 is approaching or in retirement, evaluating on Medicare sustainability, long-term care accessibility, and rural hospital viability. These are not abstract policy concerns for this age cohort; they are direct material interests that shape voting behavior when the incumbent's record on healthcare funding and system access is visible. Any candidate must be able to articulate a specific legislative agenda around healthcare system sustainability, not generic messaging about healthcare quality, because voters this age evaluate on tangible program viability." },
          { name: "Treating 76% as an Insurmountable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76% result in a demographically homogeneous district reflects strong consolidation of a specific voter coalition — older homeowners with established interests in stability — but does not reflect a district where the opposition lacks organizing opportunities. If the college-educated 29% becomes mobilized around institutional funding concerns, or if older voters perceive inadequate attention to healthcare system viability, the margin could compress rapidly. The 24% opposition represents real voters with identifiable concerns; any incumbent who treats 76% as a safe floor rather than an achieved coalition will find that coalition erodes under pressure." },
          { name: "Dismissing Democratic Opposition as Unorganizable", tag: "avoid", why: "Maine's 24% Democratic baseline in HD-10 is not an immovable floor — it reflects current voter preference, not a ceiling on what opposition organizing could achieve. In a district where college-educated professionals and healthcare-dependent retirees represent meaningful portions of the electorate, specific opposition campaigns on education funding, healthcare access, or municipal service adequacy could shift 4-6 percentage points relatively easily. Treating the opposition as too weak to merit serious engagement is the mistake that creates vulnerability in an otherwise stable seat." }
