          { name: "Treating 69% as a Durable Majority Rather Than a Contingent Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 result in a rural district of older, lower-income homeowners should not be treated as a stable floor but as a consolidation of a specific constituency whose material concerns must be continuously addressed to remain consolidated. If older homeowners become frustrated by property tax burden, rural hospital closures, or healthcare cost acceleration — or if younger residents or tribal communities become mobilized around economic opportunity or governance priorities — the margin can compress quickly. Strout's vulnerability is not the current deficit but the assumption that the 69% represents inherited partisan loyalty rather than active constituent satisfaction." },
          { name: "Positioning Rural Economic Anxiety as a Partisan Issue Rather Than a Shared Governance Challenge", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural Maine's economic pressures — aging population, limited economic opportunity, rising fixed costs, service decline — are shared challenges across party lines; positioning these concerns as partisan rather than practical governance problems alienates the very older homeowners whose economic security is the foundation of the 69% margin. A representative who frames property tax burden or rural hospital viability as Democratic attacks rather than shared problems needing legislative solutions will find that framing actively hostile to the material reality of a 47.5-year-old homeowner living with these concerns daily." }
