          { name: "Expansion Beyond the Existing 68% Through Demographic Growth", tag: "avoid", why: "The district's demographic composition — 91.21% white, only 6.2% combined minority population, and very low renter rate — means that demographic growth and minority turnout expansion are not viable paths to expanding the 68% margin. Any strategic plan that relies on capturing growth through minority mobilization or demographic shift is arithmetic fantasy; the growth that matters in this district is either consolidating higher percentages from the existing 68% (facing diminishing returns) or eroding the Republican 32%. Focusing energy on demographic growth that does not exist in the district is a misallocation of limited campaign resources." },
          { name: "Assuming the 68% is a Floor Rather Than a Renewal Contract", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated suburban voters, while reliably Democratic at the national level in recent years, have demonstrated willingness to shift to Republican candidates when they perceive specific governance failures around education quality, fiscal responsibility, or tax fairness. A 68% margin in a district with this demographic profile is not a permanent achievement but a reflection of current satisfaction with Friedmann's performance on the issues that matter most to college-educated homeowners. Any incumbent or challenger who treats 68% as a floor rather than a number that must be earned through continuous delivery to the constituency's actual priorities is making a strategic error; college-educated voters are sophisticated enough to punish perceived complacency or underperformance." }
