          { name: "Native American Community Engagement & Tribal Partnership", tag: "lean-into", why: "The four Census categories account for approximately 97.3% of the district, leaving a ~2.7% gap that in southern Maine tribal territory represents Native American residents whose civic organization and voter participation have proven capable of determining electoral outcomes in competitive races. At 52-48, the incumbent's margin is smaller than the potential swing from Native American turnout or candidate preference shift. Any candidate who wants to hold or win this seat must engage tribal governments and Native American constituents as genuine governing partners—not on separate campaign tracks—with specific attention to Indian Health Service access, tribal economic development, and state-tribal collaboration on healthcare and economic opportunity." },
          { name: "Understanding the 48% Opposition in an Aging Homeowner District", tag: "careful", why: "A 48% opposition in a rural, homeowner-heavy, 95.71% white district with a 55.6-year median age cannot be explained by demographics alone and represents either a genuine governing failure by the incumbent, successful Republican messaging on economic concerns (taxes, healthcare costs, rural service decline), strong Native American turnout for the Democratic candidate, or some combination. Identifying specifically what generated that 48%—whether frustrated older homeowners who voted Democratic despite economic concerns, Republican inroads into the homeowner base, or strong tribal community mobilization—is essential to understanding whether the 52% holds or whether the opposition's base is genuinely moving or simply mobilized." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Safe Democratic Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result in a district this old, this homeowner-dominant, and this rural in Republican-trending Maine is not a safe Democratic seat—it is a competitive seat whose current margin depends on holding specific constituencies and potentially on Native American turnout that may not be automatically replicated. Any incumbent who campaigns this district as a secure hold will find themselves vulnerable to a challenger who campaigns actively on aging homeowners' concrete economic concerns and presents themselves as more responsive to rural property tax and healthcare affordability." },
          { name: "Positioning Tribal Governments as a Special Interest Rather Than a Governing Partner", tag: "avoid", why: "Any candidate whose framing or record positions tribal governments or Native American concerns as an interest group competing with 'regular' constituents will find that framing actively misaligned with both the demographic reality of the district and the civic infrastructure that Native American communities have built in southern Maine. In a 52-48 seat where tribal voter participation may be decisive, framing that marginalizes tribal governance as a special interest rather than recognizing it as a constituent relationship and governing obligation will be actively counterproductive to holding this seat." }
