          "Address the 44% opposition directly on economic fairness and working-class opportunity: demonstrate specifically how property tax management, job creation, and wage growth serve working families, not just professionals — the 35.9% renter rate includes service and trades workers who need pathways to economic security as much as college-educated renters do.",
          "Consolidate younger renters through housing affordability and workforce development messaging: the dual-income professional households in this district have school-age children; younger renters worry about staying in Maine or relocating for opportunity — both constituencies need to see that the state is solving housing costs and creating jobs that justify staying.",
          "Do not treat the 56% as a durable baseline — at 48.4% college attainment, this district will swing back toward Republican margins if the incumbent is perceived as indifferent to working-class economic concerns or if a challenger emerges who credibly positions the college-educated professional class as the source of Maine's economic problems rather than its foundation."
