          "Engage the renter minority (29.6%) with concrete housing and cost-of-living policy: renters in a district this old are often either younger adults with limited local economic opportunity or older people on fixed incomes who cannot afford homeownership—their economic concerns are real and deserve specific attention.",
          "Avoid assuming the 62% is a durable floor: in a district with modest educational attainment and below-average college exposure, persuasion on economic grounds is possible—a challenger who offers credible solutions to household financial anxiety can move votes, particularly if the incumbent appears inattentive to material concerns.",
          "Treat this seat as a governance responsibility rather than a political asset: the demographic stability and homeowner majority mean the 62-38 result reflects the incumbent's reputation on household economic issues, not inherited partisan loyalty—active constituent service on tangible concerns is the only durable hold strategy."
