          { name: "Aging, College-Educated Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 57.2 median age, 49.1% college attainment, and 84.7% homeownership with $68,647 income, HD-48 is a constituency of older, educated, asset-holding voters evaluating governance on whether it protects their economic interests in aging. Healthcare cost containment, property-tax management, and public-service quality are the material concerns that drive voting behavior in this demographic cohort — and Democratic governance that addresses these concerns specifically, rather than appealing to abstract progressive values, is what consolidates the 66% margin into a durable coalition." },
          { name: "Quality of Public Education & Higher Education Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 49.1% college-educated adults in a district where educational attainment is the highest or near-highest marker of economic identity, education funding and the quality of K-12 and higher education systems are direct constituent concerns with measurable impacts on property values, community reputation, and the pathway for younger adults. A Democratic incumbent in a college-educated district must be able to articulate specific, detailed accomplishments around education investment and show measurable progress on student outcomes and affordability — generic appeals to 'supporting public education' will not satisfy voters who understand education policy in detail." },
          { name: "Credible Republican Alternative Challenge", tag: "careful", why: "A 66-34 margin leaves one-third of the district supporting Republican alternatives, and a college-educated, affluent, older constituency is precisely the demographic most capable of evaluating and credibly supporting a Republican challenger who offers specific policy alternatives on fiscal discipline, healthcare markets, or property-tax reform. If a Republican challenger emerges who combines mainstream credibility with detailed policy answers on the material concerns of aging homeowners, the 66% margin is vulnerable — particularly if the Democratic incumbent campaigns with complacency or abstract appeals rather than specific engagement with the concerns driving the 34% opposition." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Durable Floor Rather Than an Earned Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A two-to-one Democratic advantage in a state trending Republican is not a demographic inevitability but a result of specific alignment between Democratic governance and the material interests of college-educated, older, economically secure voters — alignment that requires active maintenance. Any incumbent who campaigns as if 66% makes re-election automatic, rather than as if every percentage point must be earned through specific attention to voter concerns, will find the margin narrower than expected and the seat more vulnerable than it appears." },
          { name: "Ignoring Fiscal Responsibility Concerns Among Affluent Homeowner Base", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated, $68,647-income homeowners are not opposed to taxation in principle, but they expect government spending to be disciplined, results-oriented, and justified through clear accounting of benefits and costs. Any Democratic candidate who campaigns on spending commitments without credible answers about fiscal sustainability or specific outcomes will find that affluent, educated voters — particularly those in their late 50s and beyond — evaluate the candidate as fiscally reckless rather than as progressive. This constituency will not reward vague appeals to investment if they perceive government as inefficient." }
