          { name: "College-Educated Constituency & Education Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 47.8% college attainment — significantly above state and national averages — ME-HD-49 is a highly educated constituency that evaluates candidates on intellectual rigor, evidence-based policy, and investment in public institutions including higher education, K-12 schools, and research. The 61% Democratic result reflects strong alignment with that party's education-funding priorities, but the representative must demonstrate command of education policy specifics: school funding adequacy, teacher compensation, student debt management, and the role of higher education in regional economic development. Generic education rhetoric will underperform in a district this educated." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity & Inclusion Politics", tag: "careful", why: "At 90.84% white, ME-HD-49 is demographically homogeneous in a way that creates both an opportunity and a risk. The Democratic 61% margin in a high-income, college-educated, heavily white district suggests strong progressive values on issues like racial justice, immigration, and LGBTQ+ equality — but a representative must articulate these values in ways that resonate with this specific constituency without triggering the backlash that sometimes emerges when college-educated white voters perceive identity politics as threatening their material interests or as dismissive of working-class white concerns. The balance requires authentic commitment to inclusion alongside economic messaging that emphasizes rising-tide prosperity." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Safe Democratic Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 Democratic result is comfortable but not dominant — and in a district this affluent and educated, the 39% Republican share likely represents a well-organized, mobilized opposition that evaluates on specific policy failures or perceived overreach. In high-income districts, swing voters often respond to concerns about tax policy, regulatory burden, or government efficiency more sharply than in lower-income areas. Any incumbent Democrat who campaigns this seat as though 61% represents a durable majority without addressing the 39% opposition's specific grievances risks an energized challenger mobilizing property-tax frustration, healthcare-reform backlash, or concerns about regulatory overreach." },
          { name: "Assuming Rural or Working-Class Priorities", tag: "avoid", why: "At $102,587 income, 86% homeownership, and 47.8% college attainment in a coastal Maine community, ME-HD-49 is fundamentally a suburban, affluent, post-industrial constituency — not a working-class or rural one. Campaign messaging that emphasizes labor, manufacturing, or rural infrastructure will sound disconnected from the material reality of a district where median income exceeds the state average by 40% and professional employment dominates. The representative must address this district's actual economic concerns: property tax equity, coastal environmental protection, healthcare accessibility for the well-insured but aging population, and the stability of professional employment and retirement income." }
