        issues: [
          { name: "Rural Aging Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46.5 median age, 81.6% homeownership, and $59,056 income, HD-68 is a community of older, asset-holding but modest-income households where rising fixed costs—property taxes, heating, healthcare—are outpacing income growth. The 67% margin reflects alignment with this constituency, but a representative who fails to actively address property tax burden, rural hospital access, Medicare sustainability, and heating assistance will find that alignment eroding. These are not abstract policy debates; they are the direct financial pressures shaping daily life for most households in the district." },
          { name: "Rural Service Viability & Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural Maine faces structural challenges in healthcare delivery (hospital consolidation, specialist access), broadband access, and school funding that create real quality-of-life gaps for residents. A 46.5-year-old, aging homeowner constituency depends on functional rural infrastructure more acutely than younger, more mobile populations. A representative who engages these issues with fluency and champions specific state investment in rural service delivery—not token acknowledgment—better serves the district and better positions themselves electorally against a challenger who might emphasize the same concerns." },
          { name: "Understanding the 33% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "The 33% Democratic opposition in a rural Maine district with this demographic profile is substantial enough to demand explanation rather than dismissal. Whether driven by rural healthcare frustration, property tax anxiety, service delivery failures, or another concrete concern, identifying what is generating the opposition is essential to either consolidating the existing margin or preparing for a more competitive cycle. Incumbents who assume 67% is a stable floor without understanding what created the 33% often find themselves surprised by a challenger who addresses that opposition's actual concerns." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Demographically Safe Without Active Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-68's demographic profile (94.72% white, older, homeowner-dominant) might suggest an incumbent can coast on inherited partisan advantage, but a 67% margin in a district this demographically Republican is actually a signal that active constituent service and governance delivery are required to maintain it. A representative who consolidates only the existing base and neglects engagement with the 33% opposition or with the aging homeowner constituency's rising cost pressures will find the margin eroding faster than the demographics alone would predict." },
          { name: "Dismissing the ~3.7% AIAN Population as Too Small for Electoral Attention", tag: "avoid", why: "While 3.7% is smaller than the AIAN populations in Oklahoma districts, it represents a distinct constituency with specific governance needs around tribal healthcare access, land management, and economic development. In a district where a challenger might emerge with stronger tribal community engagement, even modest shifts in AIAN turnout or candidate preference can affect close elections. More fundamentally, serving all of the district's actual constituents—including those not captured in the four Census categories—is a basic governance obligation." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Maine HD-68: A Rural, Aging, Homeowner-Dominant District at 67% Republican—Safe Only If the Representative Actively Serves the Constituency That Elected Them",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "HD-68 is a demographically homogeneous rural district: 94.72% white, 46.5-year median age, 81.6% homeownership, $59,056 median household income, and only 21.6% college attainment. These numbers describe a constituency of older, asset-holding but modest-income homeowners whose economic security depends on stable property values, affordable healthcare, and functional rural services. The 67-33 Republican margin reflects this profile—but the 33% Democratic opposition in a rural Maine district is itself significant and demands explanation. At this income level and age, fixed costs (property taxes, heating, healthcare) are rising faster than incomes for many households, and rural service decline (hospital consolidation, school funding pressures, broadband gaps) creates real anxiety about whether government is delivering for them. Collamore won with 67%, which is solid but not overwhelming in a district this demographically Republican. The question any strategist must ask: What is generating the one-third opposition, and what would it take to either consolidate that margin or lose it?",
          "The gap between the four reported Census categories (96.32%) and 100% suggests approximately 3.7% of the district's population is not captured in the standard demographic reporting—likely American Indian/Alaska Native residents, a historically significant population in rural Maine. While 3.7% is smaller than the AIAN gaps in the Oklahoma districts, it represents a constituency whose economic interests (tribal healthcare access, land management, economic development) and civic infrastructure (tribal governments, cultural institutions) deserve specific constituent attention rather than invisibility. The real strategic vulnerability for an incumbent in HD-68 is not a sudden demographic shift but rather governance drift: a representative who consolidates only the existing homeowner majority and ignores the 33% opposition, the aging households' rising cost pressures, and the AIAN community's needs will find that 67% margin eroding in the next cycle when a challenger emerges who takes those concerns seriously. Collamore's task is not to maintain 67%—it is to understand why 33% voted differently and to govern in ways that make that opposition smaller, not larger.",
          "Collamore should treat the 33% opposition as actionable intelligence about what is not working in the district rather than as a permanent Democratic voting bloc. Rural Maine households at $59,056 income with a 46.5-year median age are not ideologically Democratic—they are economically pragmatic and will vote for whoever they believe is addressing their material concerns. Property tax relief, rural hospital access, heating assistance, broadband infrastructure, and the healthcare cost burden for households aging into Medicare are the concrete issues most likely driving the 33%. A representative who campaigns on these issues with specific policy solutions, rather than abstract conservative positioning, better reflects the actual district and better positions themselves to either consolidate the existing margin or face a credible challenger who does."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with rural economic security: property tax fairness, healthcare affordability (particularly for aging households), rural hospital viability, and broadband infrastructure are the material governance concerns for a 46.5-year-old, 81.6% homeowner constituency at $59,056 income—and specifically address why current policies are or are not working for them.",
          "Identify the drivers of the 33% opposition with specificity: whether it reflects rural service frustration, healthcare cost anxiety, property tax burden, local governance failures, or another concrete concern—and offer direct policy responses rather than assuming it represents a permanent Democratic constituency.",
          "Engage the approximately 3.7% AIAN population (the Census gap) with active constituent attention: tribal healthcare access, land management partnership, and economic development are governance issues that affect this community, and visibility and responsiveness matter electorally even in small populations.",
          "Avoid campaigning this seat as though 67% is a safe majority—it is a solid margin earned by alignment with the homeowner majority's priorities, and that alignment must be actively maintained through governance that demonstrates concrete delivery on the issues most likely driving the 33% opposition."
        ]
