          { name: "Understanding Why the Margin Is Only 51% in a Demographically Republican District", tag: "lean-into", why: "A one-point Republican victory in a district that is 92.83% white, 79.3% homeowner, and has a 53.1 median age is not a demographic baseline — it is a political anomaly signaling either genuine economic anxiety among voters who would typically be solidly Republican, or an incumbent whose personal performance and constituent service record are the primary factors holding the coalition. The gap between demographic expectation (suggesting 55-60% Republican) and electoral reality (51%) is the most important strategic question about HD-83. A candidate who does not interrogate and address this gap risks assuming a stability that does not exist." },
          { name: "The 4.2% Demographic Gap and Turnout Patterns", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 96.01% of the district's population, leaving a 3.99% gap (approximately 3,000-4,000 residents) that could represent recent immigrants, transient populations, or other communities not fully captured in standard demographic reporting. In a one-point race, understanding who comprises this gap, their electoral participation patterns, and whether they are being mobilized or demobilized by either campaign is strategically relevant. The gap is small enough not to be a primary swing factor, but large enough to warrant investigation as part of understanding the district's actual composition." },
          { name: "Assuming 51% Represents a Durable Republican Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51-49 result is not a majority — it is a one-point plurality of contested voters in a district where 49% actively chose the Democratic alternative. Any candidate who campaigns as though 51% represents a comfortable hold or that demographic composition guarantees future Republican victory is arithmetically and strategically wrong. The narrow margin combined with the demographic profile suggests that modest shifts in messaging on healthcare, property tax fairness, or constituent service quality could easily flip the outcome." },
          { name: "Treating Aging Homeowners' Economic Concerns as a Secondary Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "With a 53.1 median age, 79.3% homeownership, and $69,574 income, this constituency's material concerns — healthcare costs, Medicare sustainability, property tax burden, the ability to age in place — are not secondary messaging priorities; they are the primary drivers of electoral behavior in HD-83. Any campaign that subordinates these concerns to abstract conservative positioning or assumes inherited loyalty from older homeowners is misreading the district's actual political economy and will find that the 49% opposition has effectively organized around the very issues the campaign ignored." }
