          "The underlying economic profile of ME-HD-87 — $71,490 median household income, 24.5% college attainment, 39.4 median age, 19.9% renter rate — describes a stable, property-owning, mixed-skill constituency of moderate income. This is neither wealthy exurban nor struggling rural; it is the economic middle, anchored by homeownership and local employment. The 90.26% white population, in the context of Maine's demographic composition, reflects a community with deep historical roots and relatively low in-migration. However, the absence of meaningful Democratic opposition in 2024 should not be mistaken for absence of genuine political vulnerability or constituent dissatisfaction. In Maine's recent political history, rural and exurban districts have proven capable of rapid partisan shifts when incumbents lose touch with constituent economic concerns — property tax fairness, rural service delivery, and housing affordability are the material issues that move these voters. An unopposed victory is a temporary advantage; the next challenger will target the same economic anxieties Boyer's absence of opposition may have allowed to accumulate.",
