          { name: "Treating 100% Unopposed as Validation", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election in a deep blue district reflects low salience and the absence of Republican infrastructure, not strong constituent satisfaction or deep attachment to the incumbent. A young, renting, working-class, Black-plurality constituency at $48,695 income is precisely the demographic profile most vulnerable to electoral volatility when economic conditions worsen. Any candidate who campaigns as though the seat is inherently safe without proactive constituent engagement on housing, wages, and healthcare access risks allowing deteriorating material conditions to erode the Democratic coalition and create an opening for a future challenger." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Generic Democratic Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "The district's Democratic alignment is real but not historically deep—it reflects current economic anxiety and the perception that Democratic messaging on wages, healthcare, and fairness resonates better than Republican alternatives. Generic progressive rhetoric about equity and justice without specific policy record on tenant protections, wage enforcement, and healthcare access feels hollow to a constituency experiencing daily cost-of-living pressure at $48,695 income. An incumbent who relies on party affiliation rather than demonstrable legislative delivery on the material economic issues driving the constituency's political choices creates an opening for a primary challenger offering more aggressive working-class advocacy." }
