          "The strategic vulnerability in HD-95 is not immediate but structural: a young, renting, working-class, Black-plurality constituency at $48,695 income is precisely the demographic profile most exposed to inflation, healthcare cost increases, and housing insecurity—the material conditions that generate electoral volatility when they worsen faster than wages rise. The 66.7% white population, while a plurality, is likely older, homeowner-concentrated, and experiencing different economic pressures than the younger, renting Black and multiracial communities. A representative who campaigns unopposed and assumes the district's Democratic alignment is self-sustaining risks becoming invisible to the most economically vulnerable constituency members until a credible challenger—either Republican with targeted economic messaging or a primary opponent offering more aggressive representation—surfaces and mobilizes them. Holding HD-95 requires proactive constituent engagement around concrete economic security issues: housing affordability, wage floors, healthcare access, and the cost-of-living pressures that 57.2% renters experience at $48,695 household income on a daily basis."
