          { name: "Treating Older Homeowners as a Monolithic Voting Block", tag: "avoid", why: "The 50-50 result indicates that this district's 50.1-year-old, $84,606-income, 74%-homeowner constituency is not voting as a unified block — they are divided. Any candidate who assumes that 'older homeowner' automatically means reliable support without understanding the specific economic pressures, healthcare concerns, and property-tax frustrations that are driving the split will fail to consolidate the constituency. The tie is evidence that older homeowners in HD-96 are evaluating based on material outcomes, not partisan affiliation." },
          { name: "Assuming Rural Maine Demographics Automatically Favor One Party", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50-50 result in a district with upper-middle income, moderate college attainment, and an older, homeowner-majority demographic is a clear signal that traditional partisan assumptions about rural Maine are not holding. Any candidate who campaigns this district as though its demographics guarantee a predictable partisan outcome — or who treats the opposition as representing an alien constituency rather than a portion of the same older, educated, property-owning community — is misreading the strategic reality. This district has moved beyond traditional voting patterns, and a candidate who operates from that understanding will perform better than one who assumes the old math still holds." }
