          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access & Cost Containment", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural Maine faces sustained challenges with hospital closures, primary care deserts, and Medicare reimbursement rates that make rural healthcare delivery economically fragile. For a $72,403-median-income constituency with a 37.6-year median age and significant homeownership, healthcare costs are a material constraint on economic security, and the viability of rural healthcare infrastructure directly affects both access and cost. A legislator who engages rural hospital sustainability, rural provider reimbursement, and Maine's Medicaid expansion as central governance priorities—not peripheral to tax policy—is addressing the lived economic concern that most directly affects this constituency's material wellbeing." },
          { name: "Unopposed Victory as Political Risk Signal", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed 2024 election in a district of 30,000+ residents typically reflects Democratic Party weakness or resource constraints rather than genuine voter satisfaction with the incumbent. In rural Maine, an unopposed win should be treated as a warning sign rather than a floor—a signal to understand specifically what economic pressures, governance failures, or voter frustrations might activate opposition in the next cycle. Any candidate who treats this unopposed result as evidence of durable support and governs accordingly risks allowing latent dissatisfaction to crystallize around a credible challenger before recognizing the threat." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture-War Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural Maine's Republican coalition is economically motivated and pragmatic about government's role in rural infrastructure, healthcare access, and tax fairness—not driven by national social conservatism or culture-war messaging. Any legislator who campaigns or governs around abortion, gender issues, or other national partisan culture concerns rather than addressing the material economic security issues that actually affect working-class homeowners in the district will find that positioning alienates rather than mobilizes the constituency. The 100% unopposed result does not mean cultural conservatism is the district's dominant concern; it may mean opposition is simply absent." },
          { name: "Taking Homeowner Majority for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73.3% homeownership rate and stable property-owning majority can create incumbent confidence that the base is locked in—a dangerous assumption in a district where property taxes and healthcare costs are rising faster than incomes and where an unopposed 2024 victory obscures whether economic pressures are eroding underlying support. Any legislator who consolidates the existing homeowner base and neglects to address the specific cost-of-living concerns that matter most to property-owning, mid-career, working-class families will find that assumption tested harshly when opposition emerges and communicates a genuine alternative on tax fairness or healthcare access." }
