          { name: "Positioning Education Funding as a Partisan Issue Rather Than a Shared Priority", tag: "avoid", why: "Maine-HD-114's 50.9% college attainment and high median income create a district where public school quality, higher education access, and student loan policy are consensus issues that cut across partisan lines — not Democratic platform items. Framing education funding as a partisan priority, or suggesting that Republican voters oppose quality schools, actively alienates the 25% minority constituency and undermines the incumbent's ability to maintain a broad coalition. The most durable positioning on education treats school quality and affordability as a shared obligation that both homeowners and renters, both college-educated professionals and families evaluating public schools, have a stake in — and positions the incumbent as delivering specific results on these shared priorities rather than as an ideological advocate." }
