          { name: "Housing Affordability and Economic Mobility for Renters and Younger Professionals", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.9% renter rate represents roughly one-quarter of the district with fundamentally different material interests than the homeowner supermajority; in a district where the median income is $103,325, renters and younger adults often face housing costs that consume disproportionate income shares despite the district's overall affluence. The 73% Democratic margin contains voters who evaluate primarily on whether Democratic policy is advancing their specific economic mobility and housing access — not on abstract values alignment. A representative who makes housing affordability a legislative priority with concrete policy remedies (zoning reform, affordability requirements, rental regulations) rather than rhetorical support demonstrates that Democratic governance serves the entire coalition." },
          { name: "Environmental Stewardship, Regulatory Competence, and Climate Adaptation", tag: "careful", why: "In a 91.57% white, 51.1% college-educated district where environmental protection is a core value commitment for the Democratic coalition, a representative's credibility depends on demonstrating genuine legislative and regulatory sophistication — not merely values alignment. The difference between a representative who advances substantive environmental policy (water quality regulation, land conservation, coastal adaptation) and one who makes symbolic commitments is the difference between a durable coalition and one vulnerable to primary challenge from a candidate claiming greater environmental competence. Environmental issues are values-driven but also concrete and measurable; constituents will evaluate on results." },
          { name: "Assuming the 73% Margin Eliminates the Need for Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The supermajority margin does not mean constituents are permanently satisfied or that the representative can govern on autopilot; 73% is a mandate to deliver on the coalition's expectations, not a license to reduce constituent contact or responsiveness. In a district where political competition has shifted from general elections to primary dynamics, the representative's risk is not a Republican challenger but a more responsive Democratic alternative if constituents perceive neglect or ineffectiveness. Active constituent listening, regular presence in the district, and demonstrated responsiveness to specific community concerns are essential to maintaining the supermajority over time." },
          { name: "Treating the 27% Republican Contingent as the Primary Opposition Rather Than Internal Coalition Coherence", tag: "avoid", why: "The Republican 27% is philosophically opposed and essentially unpersuadable; spending significant legislative or messaging capital trying to appeal to this contingent diverts resources from the actual political challenge, which is maintaining coherence within the 73% Democratic coalition around material policy priorities. The representative's energy is better spent ensuring that the district's renters, young professionals, education advocates, and environmental constituencies perceive Democratic governance as competent and responsive to their specific needs rather than attempting to incrementally peel off Republican voters who have made a fundamental choice about the role of government." }
