          { name: "Treating 57% as a Safe Majority Rather Than an Active Permission Structure", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a district with this demographic profile — older, homeowner-dominant, relatively prosperous — should be more durable than 14 points, yet any candidate who treats it as comfortable and stops actively addressing the material concerns of aging homeowners risks allowing the 43% opposition to grow. The opposition in a Republican-aligned district likely reflects specific constituent frustration with local governance or economic pressures; ignoring what drives that opposition and assuming the demographic base will hold regardless is strategically naive." },
          { name: "Viewing College-Educated Residents as a Special Interest Rather Than Core Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 23.8% college attainment in a district with $78,977 median income, the college-educated minority includes professionals, small-business owners, educators, and healthcare workers whose economic interests and civic priorities may diverge from pure property-owner positioning on issues like education funding, environmental regulation, or healthcare policy. Any candidate who treats this group as peripheral rather than core risks losing the educated homeowners whose engagement and participation are essential to maintaining electoral credibility and constituency responsiveness in a district this prosperous." }
