          { name: "Understanding & Engaging the 14.5% Demographic Gap", tag: "lean-into", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 85.5% of the district's population; the remaining ~14.5% gap almost certainly represents American Indian/Alaska Native residents whose civic participation and candidate preference is not reflected in the current 53-47 margin. In Maine's tribal geography and governance context, this represents either a latent swing constituency or an already-mobilized community whose turnout and candidate support is suppressing what would otherwise be a more comfortable Republican result. Whether the gap represents dormant political engagement or active opposition, authentic partnership with tribal governments and genuine AIAN constituent attention is a governing obligation and an electoral necessity in a district this close." },
          { name: "College Attainment & Younger Adult Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "At 20.9% college attainment and $65,804 median income, ME-HD-138 has below-average educational attainment and a working-class economic profile, but the 47% Democratic share suggests younger adults and college-educated professionals are not voting on inherited partisan loyalty—they are evaluating whether government is delivering economic opportunity and healthcare access. In a district where the dominant story is older homeowners' economic security, younger adults' concerns about housing costs, student debt, and healthcare access can become invisible, but their absence from the incumbent's constituency-building is a vulnerability a credible challenger can exploit. Specific engagement on pathways to economic mobility and healthcare affordability for younger adults is necessary to prevent the 47% from consolidating into a voting bloc." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53% result is a plurality, not a majority, and in a district with an aging homeowner base under cost-of-living pressure and a 14.5% demographic gap representing potential tribal voter mobilization, 53% is arithmetically fragile. Any incumbent or candidate who campaigns this seat as though the current margin reflects a stable base rather than a conditional alignment of older homeowners' and AIAN constituents' interests is strategically vulnerable to a challenger who offers more concrete answers to material concerns." },
          { name: "Ignoring the AIAN Swing Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "In Maine's tribal geography, the ~14.5% demographic gap represents a meaningful population whose turnout patterns and candidate preference are the difference between 53% and a competitive loss or upset. Any candidate or incumbent whose constituent engagement strategy does not include authentic, ongoing partnership with tribal governments and AIAN voters is not fully representing the district and is leaving a swing constituency either disengaged or available for mobilization by a challenger." }
