          { name: "Aging Homeowner Economic Security in Rising-Cost Environment", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.8 median age and 70.5% homeownership with $62,647 household income, ME-SD-15 is a community of middle-income asset holders whose economic durability depends on controlling property taxes, healthcare costs, and rural service sustainability. For a 55-year-old homeowner whose mortgage is paid but whose property taxes and healthcare costs are rising faster than income, these are not peripheral policy concerns — they are existential questions about whether they can afford to age in place. The 50-50 result suggests that the incumbent's approach to these issues is not resonating with a meaningful portion of homeowners; any candidate must offer concrete solutions on property tax fairness, rural healthcare access, and Medicare sustainability that specifically address fixed-income households' material constraints." },
          { name: "Understanding the 4.7% Unaccounted Population in a Tied Race", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a 50-50 district, every demographic segment matters; the 4.7% gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% represents roughly 1,400-1,800 residents whose characteristics, location, and political engagement are unknown. This population could be rural communities, younger adults living informally, immigrant households, or other constituencies whose concerns differ from the reported majority. In a mathematically tied race, the candidate who understands this unaccounted population — where they live, what their economic concerns are, and whether they are being heard by either party — has a strategic advantage over one who assumes the visible 95.3% tells the complete story." },
          { name: "Younger Adult Economic Opportunity and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 29.5% renter rate and 31.7% college-educated share signal a constituency of younger adults and recent transplants evaluating whether Maine offers economic mobility and housing opportunity. In a 50-50 seat, losing younger voters' confidence while consolidating older homeowners is a demographic trajectory toward losing seats as the district ages. Young adults in Maine face real constraints on housing affordability, student debt management, and economic opportunity outside of Portland; a candidate who addresses these concerns affirmatively rather than assuming the homeowner vote is automatic builds a more durable coalition." },
          { name: "Assuming 50% Is a Stable Homeowner Base", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50% result is not a majority and not a homeowner base that is automatically returnable — it is a tied outcome that reflects fragmenting or uncommitted homeowner voters alongside other constituencies. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though consolidating existing Republican homeowners is sufficient to win is arithmetically wrong; the homeowner constituency itself is divided, and winning requires both holding homeowner support that is actively in question and building credibility with renters and younger adults who are currently evaluating their alternatives." },
          { name: "Treating Rural Service Decline and Healthcare Access as Separate from Electoral Coalition Building", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural hospital closures, broadband gaps, and healthcare access deterioration are not niche issues for a specific constituency — they are the material conditions that determine whether middle-income homeowners in ME-SD-15 can sustain their current standard of living and whether younger adults will stay. A candidate who addresses rural service collapse as an abstract governance concern rather than as the direct driver of homeowner economic insecurity and young adult out-migration misses the single most potent electoral issue available in a 50-50 district where material conditions are visibly worsening." }
