          { name: "Education Funding & School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 30.4% college attainment and a median age of 41.8, this constituency includes substantial numbers of parents with school-age children and college-educated adults who evaluate schools as a core community asset. The mechanism by which state education funding translates into actual classroom resources, teacher quality, and educational outcomes is a concrete, evaluable policy question; a challenger who articulates a clearer or more credible vision for school funding than the incumbent has demonstrated will find this constituency persuadable, particularly if it is the source of the opposition's 50%." },
          { name: "Affluent Suburban Constituency Preferences & Governance Philosophy", tag: "careful", why: "The 50-50 tie in an educated, stable, high-income community reflects genuine disagreement about governance priorities — not low engagement or partisan confusion. The opposition's equal share indicates they have made a deliberate choice based on substantive governance concerns; the incumbent must understand whether that opposition is concentrated around fiscal policy, education priorities, municipal service delivery, or a different dimension of governance altogether. Any assumption that the 50% represents a floor rather than a contested plurality will result in the seat being lost to a challenger who takes that constituency's actual concerns seriously." },
          { name: "Partisan Activation or Base Consolidation as a Primary Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "In a perfectly tied 50-50 district of affluent, educated, engaged homeowners, partisan activation is an insufficient strategy for either holding or winning the seat. This constituency evaluates on governance competence, specific policy positions, and whether their representative understands their material concerns around property taxes, education, and municipal service delivery — not on partisan loyalty or turnout-driven base mobilization." },
          { name: "Assuming the 50% Represents a Durable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50% result in a stable, educated, property-owning constituency is not a durable coalition — it is a contested plurality where the other half actively prefers an alternative and is making deliberate governance judgments rather than following partisan cues. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 50% represents a secure base rather than a genuine toss-up will discover in the next cycle that affluent suburban voters are fully capable of switching representatives when they conclude their governance concerns are not being adequately addressed." }
