          { name: "Professional-Class Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $87,487 median household income with 54.7% college attainment, ME-SD-27 is a district of professionals and educated workers whose economic security depends on stable employment, healthcare access, and property asset protection. Healthcare affordability, wage protection, and housing cost management are the practical economic concerns that drive constituent support. A representative who delivers on healthcare access, supports wage standards, and protects homeowner interests maintains the credibility that explains the 77% margin." },
          { name: "Internal Progressive vs. Moderate Ideological Tension", tag: "careful", why: "The 77% Democratic result in a high-income, educated district may mask internal disagreement between progressive activists and more centrist professionals on economic redistribution, social spending, and regulatory approaches. The representative's task is to maintain coalition by delivering concrete outcomes on shared priorities — education, healthcare, economic security — while acknowledging ideological diversity within the Democratic base. Primary challenges from the left are possible if the incumbent is perceived as inadequately committed to progressive economic or social causes, even in a safe general-election seat." },
          { name: "Taking the 77% as Immutable", tag: "avoid", why: "A 77% margin is built on specific constituent satisfaction with specific outcomes, not on demographic destiny or partisan lean. Any representative who treats the seat as secure and deprioritizes constituent service, education funding delivery, or responsiveness to material concerns is actively eroding the margin. In a district this affluent and educated, constituent expectations for representative responsiveness and actual governance delivery are high, and complacency is the primary threat to electoral security." },
          { name: "Neglecting Property Tax and Housing Cost Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 78.86% white homeownership at $87,487 income, property taxes and housing cost growth are direct material concerns for a significant constituency segment. A representative who ignores housing affordability, property tax management, or zoning that constrains housing supply will find that dissatisfaction on these concrete issues erodes the broader Democratic margin. These are not ideological positions — they are governance outputs that affect constituent household budgets." }
