          { name: "Younger Adult Economic Mobility & Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 40.3-year median age and 21.9% college attainment rate signal a working-class adult constituency for whom wage growth, housing affordability, healthcare access, and whether government is delivering material results are the dominant concerns. With 39% opposition in a state where younger voters have shifted toward Democratic preferences, the younger adult cohort in ME-SD-33 — particularly those without college credentials, who are the statistical norm in this district — represents both a potential vulnerability and an opportunity for the incumbent to build a genuine cross-demographic majority. Concrete policy on workforce development, affordable housing, and healthcare access for working adults is more compelling to this constituency than ideology." },
          { name: "The 11% Demographic Gap & Invisible Constituents", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for approximately 89% of ME-SD-33's population, leaving an ~11% gap that represents residents whose identity, ancestry, or demographic classification does not fit the standard framework. In Maine, this gap includes immigrant communities, mixed-heritage households, and others whose economic and civic concerns are real and present but often invisible in district analysis. At 11%, this constituency is too large to govern around, and any incumbent whose constituent attention is organized only around the four reported categories is systematically neglecting a meaningful portion of the district's population." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Durable Structural Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61% margin in a district anchored in non-college-educated homeowners at a 40-year median age is substantial but not structural in a state where college attainment and younger voters have shifted toward Democratic preferences. The district is aging into its current partisan alignment rather than being replenished by younger cohorts with similar partisan preference, which means the incumbent faces a demographic headwind even without active opposition. Any candidate who campaigns as though 61% is a comfortable floor without addressing younger adults' economic mobility concerns and the invisible 11% demographic gap is ignoring the arithmetic of Maine's long-term electoral shift." },
          { name: "Abstract Conservative Positioning Without Material Economic Answers", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with $78,158 median income, 21.9% college attainment, and 77.6% homeownership is not evaluating primarily on conservative ideology — it is evaluating on whether government is delivering practical results on property taxes, healthcare access, wages, and whether their economic interests are being served. Any incumbent or challenger who defaults to abstract conservative rhetoric while neglecting the concrete material concerns of aging homeowners and younger working adults will find that voters prefer a candidate who offers specific answers to specific problems. In a district this economically moderate, ideology is secondary to efficacy." }
